    "## Step 10: Assess a real security log file\n",
    "\n",
    "Export your computer's security log to **one file** and upload it here. Do the ONE that matches your computer; you only turn in one (Windows or macOS).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Windows (Event Viewer):** open Event Viewer, expand **Windows Logs**, click **Security**. In the right panel click **Save All Events As...**, set *Save as type* to **CSV (Comma Separated)**, and save it as `security_log.csv`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**macOS (Terminal):** export recent security-related events to one file:\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "log show --predicate 'process == \"sudo\" OR process == \"sshd\" OR process == \"loginwindow\" OR eventMessage CONTAINS[c] \"authentication\"' --last 6h --style syslog > security_log.txt\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "If that file is nearly empty, use: `log show --last 1h --style syslog > security_log.txt`\n",
    "\n",
    "Then click **Upload** in the Jupyter file browser and add your `security_log.csv` (Windows) or `security_log.txt` (macOS) to this notebook's folder. Set `LOG_FILE` below to match."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "0b8de5c7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Assessed 20 entries from security_log.csv\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "LOG_FILE = \"security_log.csv\"   # Windows CSV, or \"security_log.txt\" on macOS\n",
    "MAX_ENTRIES = 20                 # keep this first version small and quick\n",
    "\n",
    "def read_log_lines(path, limit=MAX_ENTRIES):\n",
    "    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\") as f:\n",
    "        lines = [ln.strip() for ln in f if ln.strip()]\n",
    "    return lines[:limit]\n",
    "\n",
    "entries = read_log_lines(LOG_FILE)\n",
    "results = [triage_log(line) for line in entries]\n",
    "print(f\"Assessed {len(results)} entries from {LOG_FILE}\")"
   ]
  },
